-- title:
--   Ascent of positive reduction order to an enlarged local ring
-- statement:
--   Fix a field $L$, a valuation subring $A \subseteq L$, a field $F$ that is an $L$-algebra, and a field $\bar F$ that is an algebra over the residue field of $A$. Let $R$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with residues in $\bar F$: a valuation subring `R.integers` of $F$ together with a surjective ring homomorphism `R.residue` onto $\bar F$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal of `R.integers`, such that $a \in L$ lies in $A$ exactly when its image lies in `R.integers`, the two residue maps agree over $A$, and every nonzero $f \in F$ has an $L$-multiple in `R.integers` with nonzero residue. Let $x$ be a place of $\bar F$ over the residue field of $A$ (a proper valuation subring of $\bar F$ containing the constants and a principal ideal ring), with associated order function `Place.ord`. Let $N_0 \le N_1$ be subrings of $F$, both local, with $N_1 \subseteq$ `R.integers`. Assume: every $f \in N_0$ lying in `R.integers` has $\operatorname{ord}_x(\bar f) \ge 0$, and $\operatorname{ord}_x(\bar f) > 0$ if moreover $f$ is a non-unit of $N_0$ with $\bar f \ne 0$; every $f \in N_1$ has $\operatorname{ord}_x(\bar f) \ge 0$; $w \in N_1$ has $\bar w = 0$; and the maximal ideal of $N_1$ is contained in the sum of the image of the maximal ideal of $N_0$ under the inclusion $N_0 \hookrightarrow N_1$ and the ideal generated by $w$. Then the first hypothesis holds with $N_0$ replaced by $N_1$: every $f \in N_1$ has $\operatorname{ord}_x(\bar f) \ge 0$, with strict positivity when $f$ is a non-unit of $N_1$ and $\bar f \ne 0$.
--
--   This is an ascent statement for non-negativity and positivity of the vanishing order at a branch point of the reduction map of a regular prolongation: the property passes from a local subring to a larger one whose maximal ideal is generated by the old maximal ideal together with one element reducing to zero (in the application, a uniformiser of an enlarged ring of constants). It is used in the construction of localised base changes of rational node rings along a layer of constants, in [`AlgebraicCurve.NodeAnnulusEngine.exists_localizedBaseChange_of_layer_pos_localization`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.NodeAnnulusEngine.exists_localizedBaseChange_of_layer_pos_localization).
